  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Monday morning: An upper low dropping in from \nAlberta/Sask has brought colder air to the region. Temps this \nmorning are expected to drop into the teens in most places. Wind \nchills this morning and Sunday morning will drop into the single \ndigits at times.\n\nMonday afternoon through Thursday, with the upper low moving out,\ntemps should begin to rebound Monday afternoon. Afterward a slow\nwarm up and dry conditions are expected into Thursday as a ridge\nslowly approaches from the west. The one exception to all dry \nwill be Tuesday when a weak disturbance in the northwest flow \nahead of the ridge could bring a isolated snow or rain shower to \nthe region. Highs by Thursday should be in the mid 40s as a ridge \ncrests and exits the region, there should also be near overcast\nhigh-cirrus clouds due to the chinook arch. \n\nFriday onward: Ensemble groupings go off the rails here with \neither zonal or northwest flow now becoming dominant. This should \nmean warmer conditions and mostly dry. However, the uncertainty \nhere is higher than yesterday. So, expect the forecast to change \nfrom here onward quite a bit with future updates. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nNo deviations to NBM\n\n- High confidence(60-90%) through Monday night. \n\n- Moderate confidence(40-60%) in the forecast from Tuesday to\n  Thursday.\n\n- Low confidence (20-30%) Thursday night onward. \n\nTFJ/GAH",
